There is no “issue” with EBS itself, but EBS has several limitations when used with EKS — especially for StatefulSets, high availability, multi-AZ workloads, and storage scaling.

Here are the real problems with EBS in EKS, the ones that DevOps/SRE engineers face in production.

❗ 1. EBS Volumes Are AZ-Locked

EBS volumes belong to one Availability Zone only.

Why is this a problem for EKS?

If a Pod with a PVC (EBS) is scheduled to another AZ, it will fail:

❌ Pod stuck in ContainerCreating
❌ Error: volume is in a different availability zone
❌ StatefulSets cannot failover across AZs

Example:

EBS created in ap-south-1a

Node scheduled in ap-south-1b

→ PVC cannot mount.

This is the biggest limitation of EBS on EKS.

❗ 2. Pods With EBS Cannot Freely Move

Since the EBS volume is tied to one node/AZ:

Pod rescheduling takes longer

If node goes down → storage downtime

No automatic multi-AZ failover

No cross-AZ pod migration

This affects high availability.
